{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And you shall love.</b> It said it with an additive vav, to say that besides the command of Shema Yisrael, which is acceptance of the yoke of the kingdom of heaven, there is the command of love of HaShem. Further, it intends to note that fear must precede love, in accordance with the statement of the sages of blessed memory (Zohar vol. III 56a) that no person attains the category of love until fear has preceded it within him — and that is the meaning of saying <b>and you shall love</b>, besides the category of fear. And its saying <b>et HaShem</b> means that through love a person cleaves to HaShem, and that is its saying ״et HaShem.״" "<b>Further,</b> it intends that through love of HaShem a person attains this level: that HaShem unifies His name upon him in unification, in the manner of His saying (Genesis 28:13) ״אֱלֹהֵי אַבְרָהָם אֱלֹהֵי יִצְחָק,״ meaning that each one was worthy in his own right that the name of His divinity be unified upon him. And you will find that concerning Abraham the verse makes clear that he was beloved, as it is written (Isaiah 41:8) ״זֶרַע אַבְרָהָם אֹהֲבִי,״ and likewise the verse alludes here that everyone who is beloved of HaShem — HaShem is called his God in unification, and the word HaShem is drawn both before and after it." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to awaken the heart of a Jew to love of HaShem, in accordance with the statement of the sages of blessed memory (Yalkut Shim'oni, end of Parashat Noah) on the verse ״יוֹשֵׁב תְּהִלּוֹת יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Psalms 22:4) — that HaShem chose no praise from all praises except when Israel praised Him and said before Him ״בָּרוּךְ ה׳ אֱלֹהֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל,״ and then He sat on the throne of eternal kingship. And that is its saying <b>and you shall love et HaShem</b> — for this reason, that He chose to be nothing other than your God. And when a person awakens his heart to details such as these, his soul will be torn loose and dislodged from its place with a wondrous and exalted awakening of love for the Beloved, may His exalted, majestic glory be uplifted." "<b>Further,</b> perhaps the intention is to awaken a person's heart through three kinds of love that exist in the recognition of created beings, and they are: love of the good, love of the pleasant, and love of the beneficial. Therefore the word of HaShem comes for all three: <b>and you shall love et HaShem</b> — with this name He awakened the heart to the aspect of love of the good, in that He is good, as alluded to in the name, as it says (Psalms 145:9) ״טוֹב ה׳ לַכֹּל״; also the desire of the pleasant, in the secret of (Psalms 34:9) ״טַעֲמוּ וּרְאוּ כִּי טוֹב ה׳״ — for every soul that this name addresses, there is no pleasantness in all created beings that can enter into it on a par with it, and this is your sign, its saying (Song of Songs 5:6) ״נַפְשִׁי יָצְאָה בְדַבְּרוֹ.״ You will also understand in the verse (Numbers 33:2) ״עַל פִּי ה׳,״ that the soul is dislodged from the body and does not feel the separation, because of the overwhelming sensation of the pleasant from the good. So we have heard love of the good and love of the pleasant from the word HaShem. And love of the beneficial He alluded to in His saying ״אֱלֹהֶיךָ,״ in accordance with its saying (Psalms 22:4) ״יוֹשֵׁב תְּהִלּוֹת״ as I wrote above, and herein is included as well love of the beneficial, for through this we sustained the world and its fullness, as Solomon's statement (Ecclesiastes 12:13) ״כִּי זֶה כָּל הָאָדָם״ — the entire world in its entirety was created only to fulfill this (Shabbat 30b)." "<b>With all your heart and with all, etc.</b> Our Sages of blessed memory said (Sifrei) concerning this what they said — one should look there at their words. And it appears that HaShem intended in this utterance to remove resentment from the hearts of those who love Him. And that is what our Sages of blessed memory said (Mo'ed Katan 28a): children, life, and sustenance do not depend on merit but on mazal — end of quote. Now these three things are the sum total of a person's desire and craving in the world: life, children, and sustenance, and one who lacks one of them, heaven forbid, is called dead. And all three of them do not depend — to attain them — on the intermediary of service of HaShem and His love, and this causes distancing from those who love HaShem. And how much more so when two of them gather upon the lover — lack of children and poverty — his heart will turn sour within him, and even if he strengthens his heart, his heart will not heed him, for what he feels will distance him from hearing to love." "<b>Therefore</b> the word of HaShem came and commanded him concerning this matter — that he should establish the love of HaShem in his heart by three things, even though He takes them from him. Corresponding to children He said <b>with all your heart</b>, after the manner of His saying (Genesis 22:2) ״אֶת בִּנְךָ וְגוֹ׳ אֲשֶׁר אָהַבְתָּ״ and so forth, and such love is a love that fills a person's heart and desire. And corresponding to life He said <b>and with all your soul</b>, and corresponding to sustenance He said <b>and with all your me'od</b> — that he should assess all three together against HaShem, for He is better for Israel than ten sons, and the table of the supreme On High is me'od (greatly), as the Tanna said (Avot 6:5) that your table is greater than their table, and life — from Him come the productions of life, and even after the death of the righteous he is called alive, as it says (II Samuel 23:20) ״וּבְנָיָהוּ בֶן יְהוֹיָדָע בֶּן אִישׁ חַי״ — see, even after death he is called alive (Berakhot 18a)." "<b>Or</b> it may say in this manner: that the verse comes to gauge the measure of love that every person of Israel is obligated to feel toward the Creator, and it said that it is in the measure of the feeling that enters a person's heart at the time when his hand reaches to attain three things at once — as you would say of a man who was bereft of children and poor and inclined toward death, and a prophet came with the word of HaShem and announced to him that he would recover from his illness, and also said to him: Behold, your wife is pregnant and will bear a son, and that great prosperity has come his way to become rich from it. Such a man would make the love of his benefactor wondrously beyond measure, and the love would be felt in his heart with extraordinary passion because He had bestowed upon him many grades of goodness. So too does HaShem command every person of Israel to act always in His love, and that is His saying <b>and you shall love HaShem</b> etc. <b>with all your heart</b> — meaning, as though He had given you every need of your heart, and these are the children; and it was precise to say ״all״ to hint at the parallel of the parable, namely that he lacked children and HaShem gave them to him. And likewise <b>and with all your soul</b> — that He gave you your soul after you had reached the gates of death; <b>and with all your me'od</b> — as though He had given you all the wealth you desire; and it said ״all״ to hint at the parallel of the parable." "<b>Now behold,</b> one who is discerning regarding the matter of passion for HaShem — all of this is a light thing in comparison to what the soul desires toward the Creator. And I have likened this matter to a great and extraordinary man of wealth who wished to travel to a distant place because of the danger of the place he was in, and he gathered all his wealth into the purchase of a great pearl worth a thousand thousand gold dinars, and set his steps toward a distant city where his heart was set, trusting in his property and his wealth. And before he reached the city that he had intended to go to, his provisions ran out and there was no dinar in his pocket, and he was lacking in great poverty and much lowliness, for the money had run out. Now this man — even though he walks in hardship and distress, with all this his heart is joyful and his kidneys exult, for great is his substance and his wealth, and he has departed from the dangerous place and will surely arrive with song, carrying the precious stone of priceless worth. So too is this matter: a person who has merited deveikut (cleaving) to Him, blessed be He, and His love, and in Him he has gathered all his wealth and fortune, and in his eyes he disdained acquiring anything in this world because of the danger of the place, besides the paltriness of its worth, and he took HaShem as his El — there is no acquisition in the world like this acquisition, surpassing the precious stone of the parable, for who could compare to Him or who could be likened to Him. And such a person — even if he should be hard-pressed and suffering during these few days of his in this world, his heart is set trusting in HaShem, the light of the world, and his heart shall rejoice and his glory shall exult. And when he arrives at the province of his desire, which is the enduring and certain world, behold his reward is with him. And from now on the belovedness of holiness — He is the Yotzer (Fashioner), and one's deveikut in Him — is that not his children and his life and his wealth, and with surpassing excellence?"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :register {:flags {1 ["God"], 2 ["God"]}}, :notes {4 "The text reads באמרו ״יושב תהלות״ citing Psalms 22:4, whereas earlier (comment 2) the same verse was cited as the prooftext for Yalkut Shim'oni; the repeated reference to ״as I wrote above״ confirms the cross-reference to the same Psalm verse."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:16:14.729226954Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}